CS 499 Milestone 5 Narrative
The artifact I selected for my ePortfolio is the Android Weight Tracking application I originally developed in CS-360: Mobile Architecture and Programming. The application allows users to create an account, log in, record weight entries, establish a goal weight, and view their weight history. I selected this application for the databases portion of my ePortfolio because it provides a practical example of using SQLite to store, organize, retrieve, and update user information. For this enhancement, I focused on expanding the database structure, improving database queries, and making the information stored in the database more useful throughout the application.
I included this artifact in my ePortfolio because it demonstrates my ability to design and improve a database-driven application. The original application already used SQLite to store users, weight entries, and goal information, but I saw an opportunity to make the database more structured and useful. One of the main improvements was adding a user_profiles table that stores information associated with each user, including a display name and preferred weight unit. I also added a relationship between the profile and the user account so that profile information can be associated with the correct user. This allowed me to expand the application beyond simply storing authentication and weight data.
Another improvement was adding database migration support. Instead of deleting the existing database and recreating it whenever the database structure changed, I increased the database version and used the onUpgrade() method to create the new profile table while preserving existing data. This was an important improvement because it reflects how database changes would need to be handled in a real application where existing user information cannot simply be discarded.
I also improved the way the application retrieves and processes information from SQLite. The database helper now contains reusable methods for retrieving a user's profile, preferred weight unit, weight history, latest weight, starting weight, number of weight entries, and average weight. The dashboard uses these queries to display database-driven information such as the user's display name, number of entries, average weight, goal weight, and progress toward the goal. Moving these operations into the database helper made the application more organized and reduced the amount of database logic that needed to be handled directly by the user interface.
The planned enhancement supports the course outcomes involving the design and evaluation of computing solutions and the use of well-founded techniques and tools to implement computing solutions. I believe I met the outcomes I identified in Module One because I expanded the existing SQLite database and improved the way information is retrieved and used by the application. My outcome-coverage plan remains generally the same, but this enhancement gives me a stronger example of database design, data relationships, SQL querying, and maintaining existing data during application changes. The additional database functionality also provides a stronger demonstration of my ability to evaluate an existing solution and determine where its structure and functionality could be improved.
One of the most important things I learned from this enhancement was that database development involves more than simply creating tables and storing information. The way tables are related and the way information is retrieved can have a significant effect on the overall design of an application. Adding the user_profiles table required me to consider how profile records should relate to user accounts and how the application should retrieve the correct profile for the logged-in user. I also learned the importance of using reusable database methods instead of repeating database queries throughout different activities.
The database migration was another important learning experience. I had to consider what would happen to users who already had data stored in an earlier version of the application. Using a database version number and onUpgrade() allowed me to add the new table without requiring the existing database to be deleted. This helped me better understand how database changes need to be managed when enhancing an existing application rather than creating a new application from the beginning.
One challenge I faced was making sure that the database changes remained consistent with the rest of the application. Adding profile information affected the database helper, dashboard, login process, and user interface. I also had to make sure that the correct user ID was used when retrieving weight and profile information so that one user's data would not be displayed for another user. Another challenge was deciding which calculations should be performed from the database and which should remain part of the application logic. This helped me understand the importance of separating responsibilities between the database, application logic, and user interface.
Overall, this enhancement improved my understanding of how SQLite can support a larger application and how database design decisions affect other components of a software system. The process also reinforced the importance of designing changes that work with existing data rather than only focusing on new functionality. The enhanced artifact demonstrates not only that I can use a database, but that I can evaluate an existing database structure, identify areas for improvement, safely modify the structure, and integrate the changes into a functioning application.
